use std
into docread.amni

This is the `docread` command-line program: it reads documents of many formats into the `dm` document model and prints them as Markdown, JSON, a metadata summary, retrieval chunks, or the passages that best answer a question. Every module-level name starts with `dr_` or `DR_`. Helper modules are used only through these functions: `zip::zp_open(data) int` (a handle of 0 or more, or -1 when not a ZIP), `zip::zp_count(h) int`, `zip::zp_name(h, i)`, `zip::zp_size(h, i) int`, `zip::zp_is_dir(h, i) bool`, `zip::zp_encrypted(h, i) bool`, `zip::zp_find(h, name) int` (entry index or -1), `zip::zp_read(h, i, out) bool` (appends the entry data), `zip::zp_close(h)`; `xml::xm_parse(data)` then `xml::xm_count()`, `xml::xm_kind(i)` (`xml::XM_OPEN`, `xml::XM_CLOSE` or `xml::XM_TEXT`), `xml::xm_local(i)` (an element name without prefix), `xml::xm_name(i)`, `xml::xm_attr(i, local, out) bool` (appends an attribute value by local name), `xml::xm_text(i, out)` (appends a text event), `xml::xm_close_of(i) int`, `xml::xm_parent(i) int`, `xml::xm_depth(i) int`; `office::of_docx(data) bool`, `office::of_xlsx(data) bool`, `office::of_pptx(data) bool`, `office::of_odf(data) bool`; `html::ht_read(data) bool`; `rtf::rt_read(data) bool`; `textfmt::tx_text(data) bool`, `textfmt::tx_csv(data, delim byte) bool`, `textfmt::tx_json(data) bool`; `eml::em_read(data) bool`, `eml::em_att_count() int`, `eml::em_att_name(i, out)`, `eml::em_att_type(i, out)`, `eml::em_att_data(i, out)` (each CLEARS out first and then appends, so never pass a shared buffer that holds other data); `playout::pl_read_pdf(data) bool`; `image::im_kind(data) int` (0 when the bytes are not an image; it knows PNG, JPEG, GIF, WebP, TIFF, BMP, ICO, HEIC, AVIF and SVG), `image::im_read(data) bool` (adds the image's size, camera data, descriptions and SVG text); `pimage::pi_extract(data, dir) int` (writes the images of a PDF into the directory dir as files and returns how many), then `pimage::pi_count() int`, `pimage::pi_page(i) int` (from 1), `pimage::pi_path(i)`, `pimage::pi_width(i) int`, `pimage::pi_height(i) int`, `pimage::pi_kind(i) int` and `pimage::pi_kind_name(k)`; `pack::pk_is_gz(data) bool`, `pack::pk_is_bz2(data) bool`, `pack::pk_gunzip(data, out) bool` and `pack::pk_bunzip(data, out) bool` (both clear out first), `pack::pk_gz_name()` (the file name stored in the last gzip header, possibly empty), `pack::pk_tar_open(data) int` (a handle of 0 or more, or -1 when not tar), `pack::pk_tar_count(h) int`, `pack::pk_tar_name(h, i)`, `pack::pk_tar_size(h, i) int`, `pack::pk_tar_is_dir(h, i) bool`, `pack::pk_tar_readable(h, i) bool`, `pack::pk_tar_read(h, i, out) bool` (clears out first), `pack::pk_tar_close(h)`; `extra::ex_ipynb(data) bool`, `extra::ex_ics(data) bool`, `extra::ex_vcf(data) bool`, `extra::ex_code(data, ext) bool` and `extra::ex_is_code_ext(ext) bool`; `ole::ol_read(data) bool` (legacy Word .doc, Excel .xls, PowerPoint .ppt and Outlook .msg, chosen from the streams inside), `ole::ol_att_count() int`, `ole::ol_att_name(i, out)`, `ole::ol_att_type(i, out)`, `ole::ol_att_data(i, out)` (each CLEARS out first, like the eml ones), and the state `ole::ol_kind` (an int set by `ole::ol_read`: 1 Word, 2 Excel, 3 PowerPoint, 4 Outlook message, 0 otherwise). The lzma module is used through `lzma::lz_is_xz(d) bool`, `lzma::lz_is_lzma(d) bool`, `lzma::lz_unxz(d, out) bool` and `lzma::lz_unlzma(d, out) bool` (both CLEAR out first, leave it empty and add a warning on failure). The sevenz module is used through `sevenz::sz_open(d) int` (a handle of 0 or more, -1 when the data is not 7z or is damaged, -2 when its header is encrypted), `sevenz::sz_count(h) int`, `sevenz::sz_name(h, i) const byte[:]`, `sevenz::sz_size(h, i) int`, `sevenz::sz_is_dir(h, i) bool`, `sevenz::sz_encrypted(h, i) bool`, `sevenz::sz_read(h, i, out) bool` (CLEARS out first; false with a warning for an encrypted entry or an unsupported method) and `sevenz::sz_close(h)`. The document model is used only through these `dm` names: `dm::dm_reset(kind, unit)`, `dm::dm_add(kind, level, text)` with kinds `dm::DM_HEAD`, `dm::DM_PARA`, `dm::DM_NOTE`, `dm::DM_FIELD`, `dm::DM_CODE`, `dm::dm_page(n, label)`, `dm::dm_set_meta(key, value)`, `dm::dm_get_meta(key, out) bool` (appends), `dm::dm_warn_add(msg)`, `dm::dm_markdown(out)` and `dm::dm_json(out)` (both append), `dm::dm_json_str(out, s)` (appends s as a quoted JSON string), `dm::dm_chunk(max)` which fills `dm::dm_chunks` (each a `DmChunk` with fields `at` and `len`, its text as the slice of `dm::dm_ctext`, `hat` and `hlen`, its heading path as the slice of `dm::dm_ctext`, and `loc0`, `loc1`), `dm::dm_ask(q, k, out)` (appends JSON lines), and the state `dm::dm_blocks` (each a `DmBlock` with `kind`, `level`, `loc`, `at`, `len`; its text is `dm::dm_text_of(b)`), `dm::dm_locs`, `dm::dm_shift` (an int read and assigned directly: it is added to the level of every heading `dm::dm_add` stores), `dm::dm_kind`, `dm::dm_unit`, `dm::dm_meta` (one line per entry: key, a tab byte, value) and `dm::dm_warn` (one warning per line); there is no other dm function. Input and output use `os::args()`, `os::read_file(path, out) bool` (appends), `os::read_line(out) bool` (appends one line without its newline, false at end of input), `os::write_stdout(text)`, `os::write_stderr(text)`, `os::flush_stdout()`. JSON requests are parsed with the `json` library: `var d json::Doc` then `let root, err = json::parse(text, d)` (err is empty on success), `json::member(d, n, key) int` (-1 when absent), `json::as_string(d, n) const byte[:]`, `let v, ok = json::as_i64(d, n)`, `json::kind(d, n) int` and `json::format_node(out, d, n)` (appends node n as JSON text).

```amni
import os;
import json;
import dm;
import zip;
import xml;
import office;
import html;
import rtf;
import textfmt;
import eml;
import playout;
import image;
import pimage;
import pack;
import extra;
import ole;
import dcrypt;
import ofcrypt;
import lzma;
import sevenz;
```

Format codes are module constants: `DR_UNKNOWN = 0`, `DR_PDF = 1`, `DR_DOCX = 2`, `DR_XLSX = 3`, `DR_PPTX = 4`, `DR_ODF = 5`, `DR_EPUB = 6`, `DR_ZIP = 7`, `DR_HTML = 8`, `DR_RTF = 9`, `DR_EML = 10`, `DR_CSV = 11`, `DR_TSV = 12`, `DR_JSON = 13`, `DR_XML = 14`, `DR_TEXT = 15`, `DR_OLE = 16`, `DR_IMAGE = 17`, `DR_BINARY = 18`, `DR_GZIP = 19`, `DR_BZIP2 = 20`, `DR_TAR = 21`, `DR_IPYNB = 22`, `DR_ICS = 23`, `DR_VCF = 24`, `DR_CODE = 25`, `DR_XZ = 26`, `DR_LZMA = 27`, `DR_7Z = 28`.

Module state: `var dr_kind_names` is a `let` array of the names "unknown", "pdf", "docx", "xlsx", "pptx", "odf", "epub", "zip", "html", "rtf", "eml", "csv", "tsv", "json", "xml", "text", "ole", "image", "binary", "gzip", "bzip2", "tar", "ipynb", "ics", "vcf", "code", "xz", "lzma", "7z" indexed by format code; `var dr_units` likewise "part", "page", "section", "sheet", "slide", "part", "chapter", "part", "section", "section", "part", "part", "part", "part", "part", "section", "part", "part", "part", "part", "part", "part", "part", "part", "part", "line", "part", "part", "part"; `var dr_tmp byte[>..]`, `var dr_out byte[>..]`.

`dr_ext(path const byte[:], out byte[>..]&)` clears out and appends the lower-cased extension of path (the bytes after the last period that comes after the last slash), empty when there is none.

`dr_starts(d const byte[:], at int, s const byte[:]) bool` is true when d holds s at at. `dr_find(d const byte[:], s const byte[:], limit int) int` is the first index of s within the first limit bytes of d, or -1. `dr_lower_eq(a const byte[:], b const byte[:]) bool` compares ASCII case-insensitively.

The module variable `var dr_pw byte[>..]` holds the password for protected files, empty by default.

`dr_sniff(name const byte[:], d const byte[:]) int` decides the format from the bytes first and the file name's extension second: "%PDF" within the first 1024 bytes is PDF; the bytes D0 CF 11 E0 A1 B1 1A E1 are OLE (legacy .doc, .xls, .ppt, .msg); a nonzero `image::im_kind(d)` is IMAGE; "PK" 3 4 or "PK" 5 6 is a ZIP container, refined by opening it with `zip::zp_open` and looking for entries: "word/document.xml" is DOCX, "xl/workbook.xml" is XLSX, "ppt/presentation.xml" is PPTX, a "mimetype" entry whose content starts with "application/epub+zip" (or a "META-INF/container.xml" entry with an .epub name) is EPUB, a "mimetype" entry starting "application/vnd.oasis.opendocument." is ODF, else ZIP (close the handle). `pack::pk_is_gz(d)` is GZIP; `pack::pk_is_bz2(d)` is BZIP2; `lzma::lz_is_xz(d)` is XZ; the six bytes 0x37, 0x7A, 0xBC, 0xAF, 0x27, 0x1C at the start are 7Z; `lzma::lz_is_lzma(d)` with an extension of lzma or tlz is LZMA; a `pack::pk_tar_open(d)` handle of 0 or more is TAR (close it with `pack::pk_tar_close`). After skipping a UTF-8 BOM and leading whitespace: "{\rtf" is RTF; then, before any markup check, an extension of eml, mbox or msg-less text whose first non-blank line starts with "From ", or whose first 30 lines include at least two header lines among "From:", "To:", "Subject:", "Date:", "Received:", "Return-Path:", "Message-ID:", "MIME-Version:", "Delivered-To:" (any case) and no blank line before them, is EML; "BEGIN:VCALENDAR" at the start (any case), or an extension of ics, ical or ifb, is ICS; "BEGIN:VCARD" at the start (any case), or an extension of vcf or vcard, is VCF; an extension of ipynb is IPYNB; only after that: "<!doctype html", "<html", "<head" or "<body" (case-insensitive) within the first 4096 bytes, or an extension of html, htm, xhtml, xht, is HTML; an extension for which `extra::ex_is_code_ext` is true is CODE when the content passes the TEXT byte test below (fewer than 1 in 100 of the first 65536 bytes are 0 or control bytes), even when it starts with markup; "<?xml" or "<" followed by a letter is XML (HTML when the extension says so); "{" or "[" with an extension of json or jsonl, or a body that parses as JSON per extension json, geojson, jsonl, ndjson, is JSON; extension csv is CSV, tsv or tab is TSV; extension md, markdown, txt, text, log, rst, org, ini, cfg, conf, yaml, yml, toml, tex, srt, vtt, or any other content in which fewer than 1 in 100 of the first 65536 bytes is a 0 byte or a control byte other than tab, newline, carriage return and form feed, is TEXT; anything else is BINARY.

`dr_zip_text(h int, name const byte[:], out byte[>..]&) bool` reads the zip entry with that name (found with the archive's lookup) into out and returns whether it was found and read.

`dr_join_path(base const byte[:], rel const byte[:], out byte[>..]&)` clears out and resolves rel against the directory of base inside an archive: percent escapes in rel are decoded, any "#fragment" is dropped, a rel starting with "/" is taken from the root, "." segments are removed and ".." removes the previous segment.

`dr_epub(d const byte[:]) bool` reads an EPUB: open the zip; read "META-INF/container.xml" and take the full-path attribute of the first rootfile element (default: the first entry ending in ".opf"); parse that package file with `xml::xm_parse`: metadata dc:title, dc:creator, dc:language, dc:publisher and dc:date go to meta title, author, language, publisher, date; manifest item id to href (resolved with `dr_join_path` against the package path) and media-type; the spine's itemref idrefs in order. For each spine item whose media type is application/xhtml+xml or text/html (or whose href ends in .xhtml, .html or .htm), call `dm::dm_page(n, "")` with n counting from 1, read the entry and read it with `html::ht_read`. Store meta "chapters". Close the zip. Return false when no package file is found.

`dr_xml(d const byte[:]) bool` reads a generic XML document with `xml::xm_parse`: walk the events keeping the element path; for an element whose content is only text (no child elements) with at most 200 bytes of trimmed text add a `dm::DM_FIELD` block "local name: text" (followed by the element's attributes as " [name=value, ...]" when it has any); longer text of any element adds a `dm::DM_PARA` block. Store meta "root" with the root element's name. Return true when parsing found at least one element.

`dr_mbox_split(d const byte[:], starts int[>..]&) int` clears starts and, when d is a mailbox, appends the byte offset where each message begins and returns how many there are; otherwise it returns 0 (starts stays empty). d is a mailbox only when it begins with "From " followed by a byte that is not a colon and at least one later message begins in it. The first message begins at 0; a later message begins at the first byte of every line that starts with "From " and directly follows an empty line (the bytes LF LF "From " or LF CR LF "From ", the offset being that of the "F").

`dr_read(name const byte[:], d const byte[:], depth int) bool` reads one document's bytes into `dm` (which the caller has reset) by its sniffed format, returning false for formats it cannot read: PDF with `playout::pl_read_pdf`; DOCX `office::of_docx`, XLSX `office::of_xlsx`, PPTX `office::of_pptx`, ODF `office::of_odf`; EPUB `dr_epub`; HTML `html::ht_read`; RTF `rtf::rt_read`; CSV `textfmt::tx_csv` with delimiter 0, TSV with a tab byte; JSON `textfmt::tx_json`; XML `dr_xml`; TEXT `textfmt::tx_text`; EML: first call `dr_mbox_split` with d and a local list of offsets (a local, never module state, because this function is recursive); when it returns a count above 1 the data is a mailbox: for each message i from 0, at most 1000 of them, its bytes run from its offset up to the next message's offset (or the end of d); add a `dm::DM_HEAD` level 1 block "Message " followed by i plus 1 as decimal text and call `dr_read` with the name "message.eml", those bytes and depth plus 1 (raising `dm::dm_shift` by 1 around the call like every nested read); when there are more than 1000 add the warning "mailbox holds N messages; only the first 1000 were read" with N the count; store meta "messages" with the number read as decimal text and return true when at least one message was read; otherwise (a single message) `eml::em_read` followed by its attachments: copy every attachment's name, type and data out of the eml module first (reading one may replace the module's state), then for each, when depth is below 4, add a `dm::DM_HEAD` level 2 block "Attachment: <name>" and call `dr_read` with depth plus 1 (an unreadable attachment adds a DM_NOTE "(not readable: <format>)"); ZIP (when depth is below 4): open it, and for each entry that is not a directory, not under "__MACOSX/" and not named ".DS_Store", at most 500 entries and at most 256 MiB of total entry data, read it, sniff it with its name, and when the format is readable add a `dm::DM_HEAD` level 1 block with the entry name and call `dr_read` on it with depth plus 1; entries that are not readable are listed in one DM_NOTE "Other files: a, b, c" (at most 50 names); GZIP and BZIP2 (when depth is below 4, otherwise the warning "archive nested too deeply" and false): decompress d into a fresh local buffer with `pack::pk_gunzip` or `pack::pk_bunzip` (false when that fails; pack has added the warning); the inner name is, for GZIP, `pack::pk_gz_name()` copied right after decompressing when it is not empty, otherwise name with its last extension removed, where a .tgz name becomes .tar, and for BZIP2 name with its last extension removed, where .tbz2 and .tbz become .tar; sniff the decompressed bytes with the inner name and return `dr_read` of them with depth plus 1; XZ and LZMA exactly like GZIP and BZIP2 (the same depth rule and warning) through `lzma::lz_unxz` or `lzma::lz_unlzma` into a fresh local buffer (false when that fails; lzma has added the warning), where the inner name is name with its last extension removed and a .txz or .tlz name becomes .tar; 7Z (when depth is below 4, otherwise the same warning and false): `sevenz::sz_open(d)`; when it returns -2 add the warning "7z archive with an encrypted header" and return false, when it returns -1 return false; otherwise exactly like ZIP through the sevenz functions, skipping directories, reading each entry into a fresh local buffer with `sevenz::sz_read` (an entry that is encrypted or that `sevenz::sz_read` cannot read is listed with the other files instead; when any entry is encrypted, add one warning "7z: N encrypted entries were not read" after the loop, with N their count as decimal text), counting `sevenz::sz_size` against the 256 MiB total, and closing the handle with `sevenz::sz_close` before returning; TAR (when depth is below 4, otherwise the same warning and false): exactly like ZIP but through the pack tar functions, skipping directories and entries that are not `pack::pk_tar_readable` (listed with the other files), closing the handle at the end; IPYNB with `extra::ex_ipynb`, falling back to `textfmt::tx_json` when that returns false; ICS with `extra::ex_ics` and VCF with `extra::ex_vcf`, each falling back to `textfmt::tx_text` when it returns false; CODE with `extra::ex_code` and the extension of name (from `dr_ext`), falling back to `textfmt::tx_text` when that returns false; IMAGE with `image::im_read`; OLE: first, when `ofcrypt::oc_is_encrypted(d)` is true the file is a password-protected Office document: with a local buffer, try the passwords in order (`dr_pw` only when it is not empty, then the empty password, then the text "VelvetSweatshop") with `ofcrypt::oc_decrypt(d, password, buffer)`, stopping at the first call that returns 1 or -1; when it returned 1, first when depth is 0 clear `dm::dm_kind` and `dm::dm_unit` and append to them the `dr_kind_names` and `dr_units` entries of `dr_sniff(name, buffer)`, then call `dr_read(name, buffer, depth plus 1)` (the buffer is an Office Open XML package), then `dm::dm_set_meta("encrypted", "yes")` and return what that call returned; when it returned -1, add the warning "encrypted with an unsupported method" and return false; when every password returned 0, add the warning "encrypted with a password; set DOCREAD_PASSWORD to read it" when `dr_pw` is empty, or "the password in DOCREAD_PASSWORD does not open this file" otherwise, and return false. A file that is not encrypted is read with `ole::ol_read` followed by its attachments exactly as for EML (copied out first through temporary buffers, the same "Attachment: <name>" headings, depth limit and unreadable notes), and then, when depth is 0 and `dm::dm_unit` holds "part", clear `dm::dm_unit` and append "sheet" when `ole::ol_kind` is 2 or "slide" when it is 3; BINARY and UNKNOWN add the warning "unsupported binary format" and return false. Whenever this function adds a heading for an attachment or an archive entry and then reads that item with `dr_read`, it raises `dm::dm_shift` by that heading's level for the nested call and restores the previous value afterwards, so the item's own headings sit below the heading naming it. Return what the reader returned.

`dr_usage()` writes to standard error: "usage: docread md|json|info FILE...\n       docread chunks MAXBYTES FILE...\n       docread ask K QUESTION FILE...\n       docread sniff FILE...\n       docread images OUTDIR FILE...\n       docread mcp\nDOCREAD_PASSWORD in the environment opens password-protected PDF and Office files\n" and exits with status 2 through `rt_exit(2)`.

`dr_load(files const byte[:][:]) int` resets `dm` once with the first file's format name and unit (`dm::dm_reset` with `dr_kind_names` and `dr_units` of its sniffed format) and reads every file into it in order; when there is more than one file, add a `dm::DM_HEAD` level 1 block with the file's base name before each (with `dm::dm_shift` at 0), then read that file with `dm::dm_shift` set to 1 and set it back to 0 afterwards, so the file's own headings sit below its name. Files that cannot be opened add the warning "cannot read FILE"; files whose reader returns false keep any warnings it added. Right after the reset and before reading any file, store meta "file" with the first file's path and, unless its sniffed format is OLE, "format" with the `dr_kind_names` entry of that format (readers may store their own "format" meta later, but the first value stored is the one kept). When the first file's sniffed format is OLE, store "format" after reading instead: the text of `dm::dm_kind` when the "encrypted" meta is "yes" (the format the file decrypted to), otherwise "ole". Returns the number of files read successfully.

`dr_info_json(out byte[>..]&)` appends one JSON object describing the loaded model: {"format": the "format" meta, "unit": `dm::dm_unit`, "count": `dm::dm_locs` (pages, sheets, slides or sections), "blocks": the block count, "chars": the total of the blocks' text lengths, "meta": {key: value, ...} from every `dm::dm_meta` line, "warnings": [every `dm::dm_warn` line]}, with every string written through `dm::dm_json_str`.

`dr_chunks_json(max int, out byte[>..]&)` runs `dm::dm_chunk(max)` and appends each chunk as one JSON line {"loc0": ..., "loc1": ..., "unit": `dm::dm_unit`, "head": the heading path slice, "text": the text slice} followed by a newline.

`dr_utf8_cut(s const byte[:], n int) int` is the largest length at most n that does not split a UTF-8 sequence (step back while the byte at the cut is a continuation byte, 0x80 to 0xBF).

`dr_mcp_reply(id const byte[:], result const byte[:])` writes the line {"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":ID,"result":RESULT} (id and result are JSON text inserted as they are), a newline, then flushes standard output. `dr_mcp_error(id const byte[:], code int, msg const byte[:])` writes {"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":ID,"error":{"code":CODE,"message":MSG}} the same way, with msg through `dm::dm_json_str`. `dr_mcp_text(out byte[>..]&, text const byte[:], is_error bool)` appends the tool result object {"content":[{"type":"text","text":TEXT}],"isError":true or false}.

`dr_mcp_tool(name const byte[:], d json::Doc&, args int, out byte[>..]&)` runs one tool call and appends its result object made with `dr_mcp_text`. It reads the string argument "path" (missing or empty: an error result "path is required"). When `os::read_file` cannot read the path the result is the error "cannot read PATH". Otherwise load it with `dr_load` on a one-element list. Tools: "read_document" appends the Markdown of `dm::dm_markdown`, cut with `dr_utf8_cut` to the integer argument "max_chars" (default 20000, clamped 1000..200000) and followed by "\n\n[truncated at N of M bytes]" when cut, then one line "warning: W" per warning; "document_info" appends the text made by `dr_info_json`; "ask_document" needs the string argument "question" (an error result when missing) and the integer argument "k" (default 5, clamped 1..20), runs `dm::dm_chunk(1200)` and `dm::dm_ask`, and appends the JSON lines it wrote (or "no passage matched" when there are none); any other name is the error result "unknown tool NAME". A path that loads but yields no readable file is an error result made of its warnings.

`dr_mcp()` serves the Model Context Protocol over standard input and output so coding agents such as Amni-Code can read documents. It loops reading lines with `os::read_line` (clearing the line buffer before each read) until end of input; blank lines are skipped and a line that does not parse as a JSON object gets `dr_mcp_error("null", -32700, "parse error")`. For a parsed request take "method" (a string) and "id" (its JSON text from `json::format_node`, or none when the member is absent); requests without an id are notifications and get no reply. Methods: "initialize" replies {"protocolVersion": the request's params.protocolVersion string when present, else "2024-11-05", "capabilities": {"tools": {}}, "serverInfo": {"name": "docread", "version": "1.0.0"}}; "ping" replies {}; "tools/list" replies {"tools": [...]} with three tools, each {"name", "description", "inputSchema": {"type": "object", "properties": {...}, "required": [...]}}: "read_document" ("Read a document (PDF, Word, Excel, PowerPoint, OpenDocument, EPUB, HTML, RTF, email, CSV, JSON, XML, notebooks, archives, images, text) and return it as Markdown with page markers", properties path string and max_chars integer, required path), "ask_document" ("Find the passages of a document that best answer a question, with their page, sheet or slide", properties path, question string and k integer, required path and question), "document_info" ("Format, page count, title, author and other metadata of a document", properties path, required path); "tools/call" reads params.name and params.arguments (an object; missing means no arguments) and replies with `dr_mcp_tool`; any other method with an id gets `dr_mcp_error(id, -32601, "method not found")`.

`main()`: arguments come from `os::args()` (the first is the program). Before anything else, when `os::env("DOCREAD_PASSWORD", x)` with a local buffer x returns true, copy x into both `dr_pw` and `dcrypt::dc_pw`. The first argument is the mode:
- "mcp": run `dr_mcp()` and exit with status 0; it takes no files.
- "md": load the files with `dr_load`, write `dm::dm_markdown` to standard output; when there are warnings, also write each to standard error prefixed with "docread: ".
- "json": write `dm::dm_json` followed by a newline.
- "info": write the text of `dr_info_json` followed by a newline.
- "chunks": the second argument is the max chunk size in bytes (default 1200 when not a number, clamped to 200..20000); write the text of `dr_chunks_json`.
- "ask": the second argument is k (clamped 1..50), the third the question; run `dm::dm_chunk(1200)` then `dm::dm_ask` and write its lines.
- "sniff": write one line per file: its path, a tab and its format name.
- "images": the second argument is an output directory, the files follow. For file number i (from 0) the directory is OUTDIR when there is only one file, otherwise OUTDIR, a slash and i in decimal. Reset `dm` once with `dm::dm_reset` (format "pdf", unit "page"). Read each file (a file that cannot be read adds the warning "cannot read FILE"); when it sniffs as PDF, run `pimage::pi_extract` on it with its directory and write one JSON line per written image: {"file": the file path, "page": `pimage::pi_page`, "path": `pimage::pi_path`, "w": `pimage::pi_width`, "h": `pimage::pi_height`, "kind": the `pimage::pi_kind_name` of its kind}, strings through `dm::dm_json_str`; any other format writes nothing for that file. Then write every warning to standard error prefixed with "docread: " and exit with status 0 when at least one file could be read, else 1.
Any other mode, or no files, calls `dr_usage()`. When no file was read successfully (except in the sniff and images modes) exit with status 1 after writing the warnings to standard error.
